What is Git?

Git is a version control system (VCS).

It is software installed on your computer that tracks changes in your source code.

For example, you have:

project/
 ├── app.py
 ├── index.html
 └── style.css

You modify app.py several times.

Git allows you to keep track of those changes:

Version 1 → Version 2 → Version 3 → Version 4

If something goes wrong, you can go back to an earlier version.

2. What is GitHub?

GitHub is a cloud-based platform that hosts Git repositories.

Think of it like this:

Git
 ↓
Manages your code and versions
 ↓
Your local computer

GitHub
 ↓
Stores/shares your Git repository online
 ↓
Cloud/server

So:

Git = tool/software for version control
GitHub = online platform for storing and collaborating on Git repositories

